> charge.  For that matter the cost of a wand should be proportional
> to the number of charges it has that way the wand with only a "You
> wrest one more" from it costs a base fee, and each charge cost some
> fix amount.

I agree that the cost of a wand should increase and decrease with
the number of charges, but I think it's pretty reasonable that
it is more expensive to try and then buy than just to buy.
It's like a rent-to-own plan.
So the reduction in the price of the wand due to its losing a charge
should be less than the fee for using the wand.

In order for this to be implemented cleanly, it would be helpful if
all objects that have charges would have a standard number of charges
as part of their object type data.  E.g. standard number of charges
for all wands of light might be 13.  Then the cost of a wand of light
could be raised or lowered on a proper scale, depending on how
many charges it had.
This would also mean you could get rid of some switch statements
and hard-coded policy in shk.c... e.g. in cost_per_charge().

